"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 15. The surgical findings included a pathological stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension, negative surgical margins, and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion. Lymphovascular invasion was present, and no lymph nodes were removed, which widens uncertainty due to missing nodal sampling. The biopsy findings showed a progression from Gleason 3+3 to 3+4 over time.\n\nCountervailing factors include the negative surgical margins and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly limits the ability to assess the trajectory of recurrence. Missing nodal sampling also contributes to this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 50.59 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 32.89–68.30 months; its coverage has not been validated."
